st month; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.19423v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 162\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Quantitative Biology > Quantitative Methods\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 24 Nov 2025]\nL9: # Title:Beyond Protein Language Models: An Agentic LLM Framework for Mechanistic Enzyme Design\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Bruno Jacob , cite6†Khushbu Agarwal , cite7†Marcel Baer , cite8†Peter Rice , cite9†Simone Raugei L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Beyond Protein Language Models: An Agentic LLM Framework for Mechanistic Enzyme Design, by Bruno Jacob and 4 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite10†View PDF cite11†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:We present Genie-CAT, a tool-augmented large-language-model (LLM) system designed to accelerate scientific hypothesis generation in protein design. Using metalloproteins (e.g., ferredoxins) as a case study, Genie-CAT integrates four capabilities -- literature-grounded reasoning through retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), structural parsing of Protein Data Bank files, electrostatic potential calculations, and machine-learning prediction of redox properties -- into a unified agentic workflow.\nL17: By coupling natural-language reasoning with data-driven and physics-based computation, the system generates mechanistically interpretable, testable hypotheses linking sequence, structure, and function. In proof-of-concept demonstrations, Genie-CAT autonomously identifies residue-level modifications near [Fe--S] clusters that affect redox tuning, reproducing expert-derived hypotheses in a fraction of the time.\nL18: The framework highlights how AI agents combining language models with domain-specific tools can bridge symbolic reasoning and numerical simulation, transforming LLMs from conversational assistants into partners for computational discovery.\nL19: Comments:  | 10 pages, 4 figures\nL20: Subjects:  | Quantitative Methods (q-bio.QM); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite12†arXiv:2511.19423 [q-bio.QM]\nL22:    | (or cite13†arXiv:2511.19423v1 [q-bio.QM] for this version)\nL23:    | cite14†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.19423†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Bruno Jacob [cite15†view email ]\nL27: [v1] Mon, 24 Nov 2025 18:57:07 UTC (1,126 KB)\nL28: \nL29: Full-text links:\nL30: \nL31: ## Access Paper:\nL32: \nL33: View a PDF of the paper titled Beyond Protein Language Models: An Agentic LLM Framework for Mechanistic Enzyme Design, by Bruno Jacob and 4 other authors\nL34: \nL35:   * cite10†View PDF L36:   * cite11†HTML (experimental) L37:   * cite16†TeX Source L38: \nL39: cite17†view license L40: ### Current browse context:\nL41: \nL42: q-bio.QM\nL43: \nL44: cite18†< prev |   cite19†next > L45: \nL46: cite20†new | cite21†recent | cite22†2025-11 L47: \nL48: Change to browse by:\nL49: \nL50: cite23†cs L51: cite24†cs.AI L52: cite25†q-bio L53: \nL54: ### References & Citations\nL55: \nL56:   * cite26†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L57:   * cite27†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L58:   * cite28†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L59: \nL60: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL61: \nL62: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL63: \nL64: [Button: ×]\nL65: \nL66: loading...\nL67: \nL68: Data provided by:\nL69: \nL70: ### Bookmark\nL71: \nL72: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL73: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL74: \nL75: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL76: \nL77: Bibliographic Explorer (cite29†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL78: \nL79: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL80: \nL81: Connected Papers (cite30†What is Connected Papers?†www.connect